'''Bankan Tey Dogon''', at first called '''Walo-Kumbe Dogon''' after the two main villages it is spoken in, also known as '''Walo''' and '''Walonkore''', is a divergent, recently described [[Dogon language]] spoken in [[Mali]]. It was first reported online by Roger Blench,<ref>{{cite web|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615111624/http://www.rogerblench.info/Language/Niger-Congo/Dogon/Dogon%20page.htm|archivedate=2013-06-15|url=http://www.rogerblench.info/Language/Niger-Congo/Dogon/Dogon%20page.htm|title=DOGON LANGUAGES}}</ref> who reports that it is "clearly related to [[Nanga Dogon|Nanga]]", which is only known from one report from 1953.

A third village investigated at the time, Been, speaks a related but lexically distinct form, [[Ben Tey Dogon]].

